WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.780
In 1792, a chemist named Gadolin received
a sample of a mysterious black rock

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:07.460
from this now-closed Swedish mine.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.000
It was far too heavy to be coal:

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.740
it was called ytterbite, after Ytterby, the
nearby town.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.559
Nowadays, we understand the basics of chemistry.

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:18.510
We've named all the natural elements and we've
made our own.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.270
But back then, it wasn't just that there were
gaps in the periodic table,

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:24.189
it's that there wasn't a periodic table at
all.

00:00:24.189 --> 00:00:26.960
Heck, when Gadolin was given the rock from
this mine,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.130
Lavoisier, known as the father of chemistry,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:33.570
had only just published the paper setting
forward the theory of elements:

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.940
and even he speculated that those elements
might include light and "caloric",

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.260
an invisible fluid that explained how heat
worked.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.149
It was plausible at the time.

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:46.050
The way to work out whether something was
a new element was basically:

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.530
do stuff to it and see what happened.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:49.850
Which is still the technique today,

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.350
it's just that "do stuff to it" is now defined
as

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.210
"smash atoms together in a particle accelerator

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:58.239
and track the bits of radiation given out
as the results decay".

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:00.289
For centuries, though, it was things like

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:03.899
"heat stuff up and see what wavelengths of
light come out"

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:10.220
or "put different voltages of electricity
through it and see how the current changes".

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:14.299
The big problem was that the samples that
those chemists were working with

00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:16.500
weren't anything close to pure,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.790
and they couldn't know how to purify something

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.790
that hadn't been discovered yet.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.220
So they had to work out:

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.729
was that a new element that they were looking
at,

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:27.700
or just a combination of known ones?

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:31.550
So papers were published that turned out later
to be completely wrong,

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:35.420
and were corrected by others: which is great,
because that's how science works,

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.240
but it also means that a lot of people and
places

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.540
that might have had elements named after them
were disappointed.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.880
Canada, Norway, Russia, unlucky.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:45.700
Here, though?

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.740
Ah, there were discoveries to be made.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.030
Through the 19th century, Gadolin and other
chemists

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.270
found that the black rocks from this mine
had not one,

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.469
but four new elements that were completely
unknown to science.

00:01:56.469 --> 00:02:01.860
And they were all named after here: yttrium,
terbium, erbium, and ytterbium,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.869
making this small town in Sweden

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:07.259
the only place in the world with four elements
named after it.

